title: Sleep + Wakeup

files:
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/SleepyRunnerP.em.ts
  - rpi.2040:rpi.distro.2040/BoardC.em.ts
  - rpi.2040:rpi.mcu.2040/RtcAlarm1.em.ts
  - rpi.2040:rpi.mcu.2040/Idle.em.ts
  - em.core:em.utils/LedT.em.ts
  - .assets/img-103-07-01.png
  - .assets/img-103-05-02.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 26
      - mark 1 B 32
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Deep-Sleep}

      The `em.hal` bucket within the (portable) `em.core` pack&shy;age contains two complementary MCU timer abstractions:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]}&thinsp;{[cu]OneShotI} &mdash; "pausing" the CPU for a short interval<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]}&thinsp;{[cu]RtcI} &mdash; entering _deep-sleep_ over a longer interval<br>

      To motivate our {[cu]RtcI} implementation, we'll return to the (portable) {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} app analyzed earlier &thinsp;{[tr,102/07/05]}.

      Using {[cu]TickerMgr}{[bm,A]}, this program awakens from deep-sleep every second &ndash; and runs _one_ **CoreMark** iteration&thinsp;{[bm,B]}.

      <div class="em-info">

      An earlier diagram illustrates how {[cu]TickerMgr} maintains its own portability via internal {[cu]RtcI} proxies {[tr,101/08/01]}.

      </div>

      Since {[cu]FiberMgr} calls the distro's {[cu]Common}.{[cn]Idle} dele&shy;gate {[tr,101/03/05]}&thinsp;, we'll finally visit {[cu]Idle}.{[cf]doSleep} &ndash; as promised&thinsp;&hellip;

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - fold 2 4 7
      - fold 2 8 19
      - fold 2 22 51
      - fold 2 53 57
      - fold 2 58 72
      - fold 2 74 87
      - mark 2 A 20
      - mark 2 B 22
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]BoardC% Additions}

      The only distro-supplied module imported here, {[cu]BoardC} binds its {[cu]Rtc} delegate&thinsp;{[bm,A]} to several proxies.

      Adjacent to deep-sleep support within this (ARM-based) distro, the {[cu]UsCounter} delegate at&thinsp;{[bm,B]} deserves mention.

      Drawn from {[cs]'@em.arch.arm'}, this implementation of {[cu]UsCounterI} uses the generic ARM **SysTick** timer.

      <div class="em-info">

      You'll soon learn why **SysTick** comes up short in serving as our distro's optimal {[cu]Rtc} or {[cu]OneShot} timer.

      </div>

      The **SysTick** peripheral can, however, measure the passage of time at the resolution of the CPU instruction clock.

      &ensp;💡&thinsp;Use **F12** to inspect {[cu]UsCounter} in more detail.
      

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - fold 3 38 50
      - fold 3 58 100
      - mark 3 A 14
      - mark 3 B 26
      - mark 3 C 54
    focus: [3, 16]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]Rtc% Driver &ndash; Interrupts}

      Similar to the distro's {[cu]OneShot} driver {[tr,103/06/03]}, its {[cu]Rtc} im&shy;plementation manages {[cs]$R}.{[cn]TIMER}.{[cn]ALARM1} interrupts.

      <div class="em-info">

      You'll also learn why our `rpi.2040` distro can use the _same_ {[cs]$R}.{[cn]TIMER} peripheral for both {[cu]OneShot} and {[cu]Rtc}.

      </div>

      Other than using a (slightly) different interrupt source {[bm,A]}, use of {[cs]$R}.{[cn]TIMER} by the {[cu]Rtc} driver should look familiar.

      Unlike {[cu]OneShot}, {[cu]Rtc} enables {[cn]IRQn}{[bm,B]} just _once_ at start&shy;up; {[cf]*_isr$$}, however, clears {[cn]IRQn}{[bm,C]} when triggered.

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - fold 3 9 39
      - fold 3 51 59
      - mark 3 A 8
      - mark 3 B 40
      - mark 3 C 48
      - mark 3 D 60
    focus: [3, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]Rtc% Driver &ndash; Time Functions}

      Our driver must also implement two {[cu]RtcI} methods which operate upon {[cs]'@em.utils/TimeTypes.em'}{[bm,A]} values:

      {[BM,B]} {[cf]getRawTime} &mdash; 64-bit seconds&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;sub-seconds pair<br>
      {[BM,C]} {[cf]toThresh} &mdash; quarter-seconds to opaque timer limit<br>

      Like many candidate {[cu]RtcI} peripherals, {[cs]$R}.{[cn]TIMER} pro&shy;vides a pair of 32-bit registers marking time since reset.

      <div class="em-info">

      The exact protocol to read and scale these registers {[bm,D]} will vary with each distro's choice for its {[cu]RtcI} timer.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 6
    text: |
      {[ht,timer]&thinsp;Timer Design Space}

      We've hinted that properties of timer periph&shy;erals make them more (or less) suitable for {[cu]Rtc} or {[cu]OneShot} drivers.

      When characterizing a candidate timer, we'll consider four independent dimensions of the design space:

      {[DC,1]}&thinsp; **Resolution** &mdash; smallest interval it can distinguish <br>
      {[DC,2]}&thinsp; **Range** &mdash; longest interval it can represent<br>
      {[DC,3]}&thinsp; **Accuracy** &mdash; its closeness to real elapsed time<br>
      {[DC,4]}&thinsp; **Power** &mdash; energy needed to keep its running<br>

      {[cu]OneShot}&ensp;|&ensp; {[dc,1]}&thinsp;›&thinsp;{[dc,3]}&thinsp;›&thinsp;{[dc,2]}&thinsp;›&thinsp;{[dc,4]} &ensp;|&ensp; _short, precise timing_<br>
      {[cu]Rtc&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}&ensp;|&ensp; {[dc,4]}&thinsp;›&thinsp;{[dc,2]}&thinsp;›&thinsp;{[dc,3]}&thinsp;›&thinsp;{[dc,1]} &ensp;|&ensp; _long, low-power timing_

      <div class="em-info">

      Since we can't measure {[dc,4]} on {[em]PI_PICO-sim$$}, our distro uses the _same_ timer for its {[cu]OneShot} and {[cu]Rtc} drivers.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - fold 4 22 30
      - fold 4 52 100
      - mark 4 A 14
      - mark 4 B 18
      - mark 4 C 41
      - mark 4 D 44
      - mark 4 E 46
      - mark 4 F 48
      - mark 4 G 50

    focus: [4, 13]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]Idle% &ndash; &thinsp;Enter&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Leave&thinsp; Deep-Sleep}

      Our earlier pass through {[cu]Idle}&thinsp;{[tr,103/06/07]} illustrated how {[cf]doPause} took control when awaiting a {[cu]OneShot} interrupt.

      By contrast, {[cu]Rtc} drivers would want {[cu]Idle} to enter a very low-power sleep mode implemented within {[cf]doSleep}.

      To save power, {[cu]Idle} enables _other_ drivers to receive call&shy;backs when entering (or leaving) deep-sleep:

      {[bm,A]}{[BM,B]}&thinsp;drivers register {[ct]SleepCB} functions at build-time<br>
      {[bm,C]}{[BM,G]}{[cf]doSleep} invokes driver callbacks at run-time<br>
      {[bm,D]}{[BM,F]}{[cf]doSleep} directly resets&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;restarts the `Dbg*` pins

      Having made the necessary preparations, the {[cf]doSleep} function finally puts the MCU into its deep-sleep at {[bm,E]}.

      &nbsp;⚠️ Details can vary widely from one MCU to the next&thinsp;!!

  - cmds:
      - open 5
      - fold 5 36 54
      - fold 5 63 100
      - mark 5 A 18
      - mark 5 B 55
      - mark 5 C 59
    focus: [5, 14]
    text: |
      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp;%[cu]LedT% &ndash; Deep-Sleep Callbacks}

  - cmds:
      - view 6
    text: |
